-- NEGATIVE fixture for the rule-date interval table (spec §15.3, R10).
--
-- The arms are ASCENDING, so first-match L4 semantics make arms 2 and 3 dead:
-- any rule date at or after 1994-04-01 is caught by arm 1. Derived half-open
-- intervals would be EMPTY, and an empty interval is a rule that can never
-- fire -- not an artifact worth shipping. So the exporter refuses with
-- D-DATEDCHAIN (Blocking) and falls back to the ordinary boolean-column table,
-- which is sound but un-analysable.
--
-- Precedent: the OpenFisca backend refuses the same shape outright
-- ("L4.OpenFisca.Lower":583-585, `dated-formula BRANCH arms must be in
-- strictly-descending date order`). This backend cannot refuse outright -- a
-- DMN export must still emit every decision -- so it refuses the TABLE SHAPE
-- and says so.
--
-- NEVER added to `goldenSubjects`: no .dmn is emitted for it, so
-- etc/validate-dmn.mjs is unaffected. Asserted from jl4/tests/DmnExport.hs.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `misordered dated chain`

TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

GIVETH A DATE
`GST commenced` MEANS Date 1 4 1994

GIVETH A DATE
`the 2023 rate change` MEANS Date 1 1 2023

GIVETH A DATE
`the 2024 rate change` MEANS Date 1 1 2024

ASSUME `no GST rate exists before commencement` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN change IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the rules in force include` change MEANS
    `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST change

GIVETH A NUMBER
`GST rate percent` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the rules in force include` `GST commenced`        THEN 3
           IF ^                            `the 2023 rate change` THEN 8
           IF ^                            `the 2024 rate change` THEN 9
           OTHERWISE `no GST rate exists before commencement`
